<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Llantwit Major</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Llanilltud Fawr</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Llantwit Major is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vale-of-glamorgan">Vale of Glamorgan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, whose Welsh name, Llanilltud Fawr, recalls the monastery founded there by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illtud">Saint Illtud</a> around 500. It was one of the great centres of learning of early Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and its church holds a collection of early medieval inscribed crosses.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/llantwit-major.jpg" alt="Llantwit Major" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Glyn Baker · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Llantwit_Major,_East_Street.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Llantwit Major lies on the Bristol Channel coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vale-of-glamorgan">Vale of Glamorgan</a>, about 15 miles from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardiff">Cardiff</a>, and had 9,530 people in 2021. The epithet Fawr distinguishes it from Llantwit Fardre near Pontypridd and Llantwit Minor near Neath. The site has been inhabited for over 3,000 years, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> times, and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> fort stands above Colhugh Beach. Near the parish church are the faint earthworks of the Roman villa at Caermead, discovered in 1888, known for its mosaic floors and finds of Samian ware and coins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximinus-thrax">Maximinus Thrax</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victorinus">Victorinus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-i">Constantius Chlorus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illtud">Saint Illtud</a> is said to have been born in Brittany and to have fought as a soldier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-cadoc">Saint Cadoc</a> persuaded him to renounce that life. He oversaw the growth of a monastery and college, Bangor Illtyd, on the Ogney Brook close to the present church, which became the first great centre of Celtic Christianity and teaching. At its height it is said to have had over 2,000 students, among them princes, clergy and saints. The 18th-century writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iolo-morganwg">Iolo Morganwg</a> claimed an older college of Theodosius there, but his works have been discredited.</p><p>The college suffered in the invasions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>, was destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> in 987 and again by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> in the late 11th century, and is recorded as restored in 1111. Around 1130, after the area became part of the Norman kingdom of Glamorgan, the church and school became the property of Tewkesbury Abbey. It continued as a monastic school until the dissolution of the monasteries under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>, becoming independent of Tewkesbury in 1539. The present St Illtyd's Church was built between 950 and 1400 and is a Grade I listed building.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In the 20th century the town grew about fifteen times in size to house personnel of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> base at St Athan. It keeps narrow winding streets, an old town hall and several inns and houses of the 16th century, among them the Old Swan Inn. Its coast forms part of the Glamorgan Heritage Coast.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Llantwit Major” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The monasteries of <b>Llantwit Major</b>, founded by St Illtud, and Llancarfan, founded by St Cadoc, were leading centres of learning.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-glywysing">Kingdom of Glywysing</a></li></ul></section>
